mso-border-left-alt:0.5000pt solid rgb(184,198,209);border-right:1.0000pt solid rgb(184,198,209);mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid rgb(184,198,209);
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid rgb(184,198,209);border-bottom:1.0000pt solid rgb(184,198,209);
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid rgb(184,198,209);"><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0.0000pt;line-height:112%;"><span style="font-family:Arial;mso-fareast-font-family:'Noto Sans CJK SC';mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';
line-height:107%;color:rgb(36,52,71);mso-ansi-font-weight:normal;
font-size:8.0000pt;">Higher cost; product restrictions may apply</span><span style="font-family:Arial;mso-fareast-font-family:'Noto Sans CJK SC';mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';
color:rgb(36,52,71);font-size:10.5000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p></td></tr></tbody></table>
<!--kg-card-end: html-->
<p>&nbsp;USPS, UPS and FedEx are familiar names to US customers, but the final-mile carrier depends on the selected route and current service arrangement. A cross-border line may hand a parcel to a local partner after US entry. Sellers should verify the displayed route, tracking coverage, destination limitations and current estimated time rather than promise a carrier name before confirming availability.</p><p>For an honest delivery promise, test the full timeline with a real order. Record the supplier purchase date, warehouse receipt date, inspection completion, parcel dispatch, first US scan and delivery date. This gives the store a defensible estimate instead of a copied “7–10 days” claim.</p><h2 id="5-direct-china-fulfillment-vs-us-warehouse-fulfillment"><strong>5. Direct China Fulfillment vs. US Warehouse Fulfillment</strong></h2>
